import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Language as data","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/explanation/language-as-data.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/explanation/language-as-data.md"}`),a={name:`handl/explanation/language-as-data.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="language-as-data" tabindex="-1">Language as data <a class="header-anchor" href="#language-as-data" aria-label="Permalink to “Language as data”">​</a></h1><p>On the handl platform, <strong>a language is a value</strong>. The tokens, grammar rules, AST shapes, eval schemes, and type rules that define a language are ordinary data — YAML fragments merged into a <code>Language</code> object — not code compiled into an engine.</p><p>This is the platform&#39;s second big idea after the closed kernel (<a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/host/blob/project/adrs/0044-language-as-data-and-runtime-split.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ADR-0044</a>, &quot;language as data and runtime split&quot;).</p><h2 id="the-language-value" tabindex="-1">The <code>Language</code> value <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-language-value" aria-label="Permalink to “The Language value”">​</a></h2><p>The <code>handl-language</code> crate (in the <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/kernel" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>kernel</code> repo</a>) defines the schema. A <code>Language</code> is assembled by merging fragment tables:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>fragment</th><th>what it declares</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>tokens</code></td><td>the token vocabulary and its tooling categories</td></tr><tr><td><code>lexer_rules</code></td><td>how source text becomes tokens</td></tr><tr><td><code>grammar</code></td><td>how tokens become AST — binding powers, child shapes</td></tr><tr><td><code>ast</code></td><td>the shape of each node kind — named child roles, arity</td></tr><tr><td><code>eval</code></td><td>how each node kind becomes a <code>Term</code> in the calculus</td></tr><tr><td><code>types</code></td><td>the typing schemes per node kind</td></tr><tr><td><code>transforms</code></td><td>rewrite and desugar rules</td></tr><tr><td><code>caps</code>, <code>capabilities</code>, <code>globals</code>, <code>options</code>, <code>representations</code>, <code>value_types</code></td><td>capability wiring, globals, option schemas, value representations</td></tr></tbody></table><p>None of these fragments is privileged. A host assembles the <code>Language</code> it needs by merging whichever fragments apply; the canonical eval-path fragment set is published as data in <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/kit-prelude-lang" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>kit-prelude-lang</code></a>.</p><h2 id="engines-dispatch-on-data" tabindex="-1">Engines dispatch on data <a class="header-anchor" href="#engines-dispatch-on-data" aria-label="Permalink to “Engines dispatch on data”">​</a></h2><p>The <code>engine-*</code> crates are <strong>generic machinery</strong>: each is an abstraction crate for one operation — <code>hnl-engine-lex</code>, <code>hnl-engine-parse</code>, <code>hnl-engine-transform</code>, <code>hnl-engine-exec</code> — plus concrete engines like the tree-walk interpreter (<code>hnl-engine-exec-tree-walk</code>). They share one rule:</p><blockquote><p>Dispatch on node kind via <code>Language</code> data — never a hardcoded <code>match</code> on kind strings.</p></blockquote><p>What is allowed to be fixed in Rust is only the calculus vocabulary: the combinator set, primitive forms, and value shapes. Adding a language construct is a YAML fragment change; adding a calculus combinator is an engine edit. The two almost never happen together.</p><p>The same discipline applies to AST layout: engines resolve child positions by <strong>named role</strong> from the <code>ast</code> fragment&#39;s <code>AstShapeDef</code> (ADR- 0081), not by positional index or kind checks — so renamed or reshaped languages run on untouched engines.</p><h2 id="what-this-buys" tabindex="-1">What this buys <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-buys" aria-label="Permalink to “What this buys”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>One engine, many languages.</strong> Lexing, parsing, desugaring, and evaluation are parameterized by the <code>Language</code> value. A DSL, a config language, or a language variant is assembled by merging fragments — no fork, no recompile of the engine.</li><li><strong>Per-workspace variants.</strong> Because a <code>Language</code> is data, different projects can pin different fragment sets and evolve independently on the same machinery.</li><li><strong>Tooling agrees by construction.</strong> A formatter, a linter, an LSP server, and an interpreter that consume the same <code>Language</code> value cannot drift apart — there is one definition.</li><li><strong>A foreign project can emit language fragments.</strong> Vocabularies are data with a stable schema, so external tools can generate rows that the engines execute directly.</li></ul><h2 id="where-the-boundary-sits" tabindex="-1">Where the boundary sits <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-the-boundary-sits" aria-label="Permalink to “Where the boundary sits”">​</a></h2><p>The engine layer is deliberately small:</p><ul><li><strong>Data</strong> — everything specific to a language lives in fragments.</li><li><strong>Calculus</strong> — the six-combinator vocabulary lives in the <a href="./arrow-calculus-kernel.html">kernel</a>.</li><li><strong>Engines</strong> — the generic dispatch machinery lives in the <code>engine-*</code> repos.</li><li><strong>Host</strong> — composition, kits, options, and capabilities live in the <a href="./platform-architecture.html">platform</a>.</li></ul><p>The canonical surface language built on this machinery is not yet released; the machinery itself is what these docs cover.</p>`,18)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};